--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@ The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),
 - Merge hook: merges through the API get the same decision as `gh pr merge` (#107): `gh api` `PUT` (`-X` / `--method`, any flag order) to `repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/N/merge`, pinned by its `sha` field, and `gh api graphql` `mergePullRequest` / `enqueuePullRequest`, pinned by the `expectedHeadOid` among that field's arguments; `enablePullRequestAutoMerge` is refused like `--auto`; a `repositories/ID/…/merge` endpoint only warns. An API merge the hook cannot fully read is refused in a gated repository, its context resolved first (fail closed): an endpoint from a variable or substitution (also a GraphQL mutation posted to `"$EP"`), a body from `--input`, a `-F` field from a file, a query from a variable, a field given more than once (gh sends `-f` fields before `-F` ones whatever their order), and a `$variable` `expectedHeadOid` at `/graphql` or a full URL (gh groups fields into `variables` only for the endpoint `graphql`). The refusal points to `gh pr merge --match-head-commit <sha>` or a literal `gh api -X PUT repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/N/merge -f sha=<full sha>`; outside a gated repository it still warns.
 
 ### Fixed
+- OpenRouter jobs take no `sources` (#146): `providers.openrouter.deny_repos` checks only the job's repository, so a source file from a denied repository, or another job's report about one, could reach the third-party provider. `job_start` (and a milestone task's planned sources) now refuses any sources on an `openrouter:<slug>` model before a snapshot is taken or budget reserved, until a source policy exists.
 - The guard refuses `scp`, `sftp` and a remote-reaching `rsync` (#116): only `ssh` was on the out-of-scope list, so a job could copy data to or from a remote host with the operator's keys. `scp` and `sftp` are refused outright (behind wrappers, as a path, in `bash -c`, and where another program runs them, exactly like `ssh`); `rsync` is refused when an `-e`/`--rsh` option (any value) or a `host:path`-shaped argument (`user@host:/x`, `host::module`, `rsync://…`, a ':' before any '/' — `./a:b` stays local) can take it to a remote host. A purely local `rsync`, both paths local, stays allowed: jobs use it inside their worktree. The refusal names the tool and says a job never reaches remote hosts. Docs list them now (skills/agent-jobs/SKILL.md, docs/GUIDE.md, docs/GUIDE.ru.md). Review fix loop: the same rsync test covers the positions where another program runs it — the command of `find -exec`/`-execdir`/`-ok` and the positional of the exec runners (`setsid`, `ionice`, `chroot`, …), with its argv up to find's `;`/`+` terminator — and `rsync` under `xargs`, `parallel` or `entr` is refused outright, its extra arguments coming from stdin; rsync's value-taking options (`--exclude`, `-f`, `-T`, `-B`, …) are consumed first, so a local `rsync --exclude cache:old` is no longer refused for its option values, while `-avze ssh` still is.
 - Codex brief lint: a bare curl/wget verb without a URL and post-verb negation (#141). Since #133 / PR #140 the network warning fired only on an explicit http(s) URL, so "run curl", «выполни wget», "use curl" went unnoticed; a curl/wget word after an action verb (run, use, execute, fetch, «запусти», «выполни», «используй», up to two words between, e.g. "fetch it with curl") now warns with the same line as the URL rule — one line per conflicting category, duplicate lines deduplicated, and «curl the fixture» or a bare «curl» still passes: without an action verb the word alone is not a fetch request. Negation is now detected after the verb too, within the same clause and only for the listed negation words right after the verb or through a linking verb (is/are/was/быть): "commit nothing", "curl is not needed", «curl не нужен», «пушить не надо»; "commit is not allowed" stays a known-limit warning ("not allowed" is not a listed negation), a bare "no" is not a post-verb negation ("run curl no matter what" is a positive request), and the same verbs keep the pre-verb negation.
 - OpenRouter jobs no longer stop at claude's own budget (#148): claude priced the OpenRouter slug by its fallback Claude price table (`unrecognized_model`) and ended a job with `error_max_budget_usd` about 15x before its real cost reached `max_budget_usd`. Their claude argv no longer carries `--max-budget-usd`; the job's proxy alone enforces the budget at OpenRouter's real price and the job still fails with `openrouter budget reached`. The recorded cost and `daily_cap_usd` accounting are unchanged; claude jobs keep `--max-budget-usd <job budget>`.
