+- Refused: kind `review` (and the gate kinds), runner codex, and repositories in `deny_repos` (an absolute entry is a path or glob of the repository path, any other a name or glob of its folder name). An OpenRouter job takes no `sources` (#146): `deny_repos` covers only the job's repository, so a source file or job report would bypass it. An OpenRouter job is never resumed or auto-continued: start a new one with what it left.
 - **Routing (`routing`, per model `model_routing`).** `sort` `price` (default) | `throughput` | `latency`; `min_quantization` (default `fp8`; an endpoint below it or with an unknown quantization is not used; `any` turns the filter off); `data_collection` `deny` (default) | `allow`; `zdr` (default false); `min_uptime_pct` (default 95, the lower of the endpoint's 30-minute and 1-day uptime); `ignore` and `order` (provider slugs). The cheapest endpoints are often fp4, hence the fp8 default; on 2026-11-04 `moonshotai/kimi-k3.1-pro` had no endpoint at fp8 or better (only fp4/mxfp4/unknown), so it needs a `model_routing` override or every job on it is refused at start.
 - **How routing is applied.** Claude Code sends no OpenRouter `provider` object. The `:floor` / `:nitro` model variants only choose a sort (price / throughput) and widen the pool with flex / priority tiers, and the account preferences (privacy settings) cover data policy and ignored providers account-wide, with no quantization and nothing per job ([provider selection](https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/routing/provider-selection), [floor](https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/routing/model-variants/floor)). OpenRouter's Anthropic-compatible `POST /api/v1/messages` does accept a `provider` object (`MessagesRequest.provider` in its [OpenAPI spec](https://openrouter.ai/docs/openapi/openapi.yaml)). So the wrapper runs a **loopback proxy per job** (127.0.0.1, a kernel-picked port, closed when claude exits): claude gets `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` = the proxy and a random per-job `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`; the proxy checks that token, sets the request's `model` to the slug and its `provider` object to the policy (`sort`, `quantizations`, `data_collection`, `zdr`, `ignore` plus the endpoints below the uptime floor, `order` = the policy's order followed by the eligible endpoints ranked cheapest first, fallbacks allowed inside those filters), adds `session_id` (OpenRouter's sticky routing, so the prompt cache stays warm), and forwards it with the real key. The ranking reads the public endpoints list (`/api/v1/models/<slug>/endpoints`, no key) when the job starts; `stderr.log` names the endpoint the policy picks.
 - **The key stays in the wrapper.** claude's environment loses every `ANTHROPIC_*` variable, the subscription's OAuth variables (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH*`), the cloud-provider switches and credentials and `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`; it gets `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`, `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` (the proxy token), `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=` (empty), and `ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_{FABLE,OPUS,SONNET,HAIKU}_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL` and `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` set to the slug. A request that reaches anything but the proxy carries no usable credential, and the proxy refuses any other token, so a misconfigured job fails instead of falling back to the subscription. A repository whose `.claude/settings*.json` sets one of those variables (or `apiKeyHelper`) is refused at start. The key is in no file the job can read and no log line. With `providers.openrouter` set, **every** job of the agent (openrouter or not) is kept from reading `key_file`'s directory by the kernel: the wrapper starts each process of the job (the runner, its fallback, the acceptance command, the prepare and manifest-check steps, the wrapper's own git) on a thread restricted with Linux Landlock (kernel 5.13+), and every descendant inherits it, so a `cat`, a script the job wrote, another interpreter, a copy, a hard link or `/proc/self/root/...` all get `Permission denied`; only the wrapper (its proxy) reads the key, before the job starts. Landlock can only grant, so the sandbox grants reading beneath every other entry along the path to the key directory: a file created later directly inside one of the key directory's ancestors (`/`, `/var`, `/var/lib`, ...) is unreadable to a job until its next launch. That is why the key directory may not be under `$HOME` (claude rewrites `~/.claude.json` there), a temp dir or the state dir; without Landlock (another OS, a kernel without it) or with such a placement, no job starts while `providers.openrouter` is set. **Codex jobs:** codex's native sandbox is bwrap, which cannot start inside any Landlock domain: the kernel forbids mount(2) to a Landlock-restricted process whatever the ruleset allows (on kernel 6.8, even a ruleset that handles only the execute right and grants it beneath `/` gives `bwrap: Failed to make / slave: Operation not permitted`). `providers.openrouter.codex_policy` decides what a codex job on the native sandbox does while `providers.openrouter` is set: `allow_unprotected` (the default) runs its codex process outside the key sandbox, so codex and every command it runs **can read `key_file`** (stderr.log of each such job starts with `WARNING: openrouter key protection is OFF for this codex job's runner`, and `system_doctor` lists the risk in `warnings`, `codex_sandbox.openrouter_key_protection: unprotected`); the wrapper's own steps of the job (its git, prepare, acceptance, manifest-check) stay inside the key sandbox. `refuse` fails such a job at start (`openrouter key protection`; a `system_doctor` problem). `codex_sandbox: "worktree-full-access"` codex jobs run inside the key sandbox under either policy (`protected`). The guard of every claude job also refuses reading `key_file` (`--no-read`), and the `sk-or-v1-...` shape is redacted from reports. The proxy forwards exactly `POST /v1/messages` and `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`; any other path or method gets 403 and never reaches OpenRouter.
